 ### Random Pose Perturbation
-Random Pose Perturbation generates random pose perturbations from the SE(3) group biased around a reference pose to ensure feature visibility and uniform coverage of the local pose space [S2]. It samples rotation axes uniformly on a unit-2 sphere in the neighborhood of a reference axis $ê$ as $ê + Δê$ [S2]. It samples rotation angles from a non-uniform distribution in $[0, \theta]$ for small $\theta$ to yield uniform random rotation matrices [S2]. These rotation matrices are converted to Gibbs vectors (q) for orientation representation [S2]. Translation components are sampled from a bounded uniform random distribution along each axis, covering transverse and forward motions [S2]. If tracking of at least four features is unsuccessful, the method re-initializes with conservative pose samples [S2].
+Random Pose Perturbation generates random pose perturbations from the SE(3) group biased around a reference pose to ensure feature visibility and uniform coverage of the local pose space [S2]. It samples rotation axes uniformly on a unit-2 sphere in the neighborhood of a reference axis $\tilde{e}$ as $\tilde{e} + \Delta \tilde{e}$ [S2]. It samples rotation angles from a non-uniform distribution in $[0, \theta]$ for small $\theta$ to yield uniform random rotation matrices [S2]. These rotation matrices are converted to Gibbs vectors (q) for orientation representation [S2]. Translation components are sampled from a bounded uniform random distribution along each axis, covering transverse and forward motions [S2]. If tracking of at least four features is unsuccessful, the method re-initializes with conservative pose samples [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 **Time Complexity:**
-- The expected number of iterations to find a valid pair depends on the density of training poses and the thresholds $\theta$ and $d$ [S1].
+Not given in the sources.
 
 **Memory Complexity:**
-- $O(1)$ auxiliary space beyond the storage of the training poses $P$ [S1].
+Not given in the sources.
 
 **Strengths:**
-- Ensures rendered frames are within observed regions, preventing failures on unobserved parts [S1].
+Not given in the sources.
 
 **Limitations:**
